## [Introduction to denial of service attacks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/introduction-denial-of-service-attacks.md)

### [Infrastructure layer attacks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/infrastructure-layer-attacks.md)

The most common DDoS attacks, User Datagram Protocol (UDP) reflection attacks and SYN floods, are infrastructure layer attacks.

- [UDP reflection attacks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/udp-reflection-attacks.md): UDP reflection attacks exploit the fact that UDP is a stateless protocol.
- [SYN flood attacks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/syn-flood-attacks.md): When a user connects to a Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) service, such as a web server, their client sends a SYN packet.
- [TCP middlebox reflection](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/tcp-middlebox-reflection.md): This relatively new attack vector was first disclosed in an academic whitepaper in August 2021 that explained how TCP non-compliance in network firewalls could result in these being tricked into becoming a TCP amplification vector.
- [Application layer attacks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/application-layer-attacks.md): An attacker might target the application itself by using a layer 7—application layer—attack.


## [Mitigation techniques](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/mitigation-techniques.md)

### [Best practices for DDoS mitigation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/best-practices-for-ddos-mitigation.md)

In the following sections, each of the recommended best practices for DDoS mitigation is described in more depth.

- [Infrastructure layer defense (BP1, BP3, BP6, BP7)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/infrastructure-layer-defense-bp1-bp3-bp6-bp7.md): In a traditional datacenter environment, you can mitigate infrastructure layer DDoS attacks by using techniques like overprovisioning capacity, deploying DDoS mitigation systems, or scrubbing traffic with the help of DDoS mitigation services.
- [Amazon EC2 with Auto Scaling (BP7)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/amazon-ec2-with-auto-scaling-bp7.md): Another way to mitigate both infrastructure and application layer attacks is to operate at scale.
- [AWS Lambda and Serverless Compute (BP6)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/aws-lambda-and-serverless-compute-bp6.md): AWS Lambda automatically scales by running additional instances of your functions in response to incoming requests.
- [Elastic Load Balancing (BP6)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/elastic-load-balancing-bp6.md): Large DDoS attacks can overwhelm the capacity of a single Amazon EC2 instance.

### [Use AWS Edge locations for scale (BP1, BP3)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/use-aws-edge-locations-for-scale-bp1-bp3.md)

Access to highly scaled, diverse internet connections can significantly increase your ability to optimize latency and throughput to users, to absorb DDoS attacks, and to isolate faults while minimizing the impact on your application's availability.

- [Web application delivery at the edge (BP1)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/web-application-delivery-at-the-edge-bp1.md): You can use Amazon CloudFront to deliver your entire website including static, dynamic, streaming, and interactive content.
- [Protect network traffic further from your origin using AWS Global Accelerator (BP1)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/protect-network-traffic-further-from-your-origin-using-aws-global-accelerator-bp1.md): You can implement a DDoS resilient architecture that provides many of the same benefits as the web application delivery at the edge best practices, even if your application uses protocols not supported by CloudFront or you're operating a web application that requires global static IP addresses.
- [Domain name resolution at the edge (BP3)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/domain-name-resolution-at-the-edge-bp3.md)

### [Application layer defense (BP1, BP2)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/application-layer-defense-bp1-bp2.md)

Many of the techniques discussed so far in this paper are effective at mitigating the impact that infrastructure layer DDoS attacks have on your application's availability.

### [Detect and filter malicious web requests (BP1, BP2)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/detect-and-filter-malicious-web-requests-bp1-bp2.md)

When your application runs on AWS, you can use Amazon CloudFront (and its HTTP caching capability), AWS WAF, and Shield Advanced Automatic Application layer protection to help prevent unnecessary requests reach your origin during application layer DDoS attacks.

- [Amazon CloudFront](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/cloudfront.md): Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) that helps protect HTTP/S-based applications in the following ways:

### [AWS WAF](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/aws-waf.md)

By using AWS WAF, you can configure web access control lists (web ACLs) on your global CloudFront distributions or regional resources to filter, monitor and block requests based on request signatures.

- [AWS WAF – Rate-based rules](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/aws-waf-rate-based-rules.md): AWS strongly recommends protecting against HTTP request floods by using rate-based rules in AWS WAF to automatically block or challenge requests matching a specific aggregation key (which is by IP address), when the number of requests received in a configurable sliding window exceed a threshold that you define.
- [AWS WAF – ASN matching feature](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/aws-waf-asn-matching.md): By monitoring and restricting traffic from specific ASNs, you can mitigate risks associated with malicious actors, comply with regulatory requirements, and optimize the performance and availability of your web applications.
- [AWS WAF – IP reputation rule groups](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/aws-waf-ip-reputation.md): To help mitigate attacks based on IP address reputation, you can create rules that match IPs making HTTP requests to custom IPSets that you maintain or use AWS WAF Managed IP Reputation rule groups.
- [AWS WAF – Block access to unused URI paths and file extensions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/aws-waf-block-unused-uri.md): Block access to unused URI paths using AWS WAF.
- [AWS WAF – Challenge rule action](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/aws-waf-challenge-rule-action.md): The Challenge rule action provides an effective defense against DDoS botnets, which typically use basic scripts designed to maximize target impact while minimizing operational costs.
- [AWS WAF – Intelligent threat mitigation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/aws-waf-intelligent-threat-mitigation.md): Botnets are a serious security threat and are commonly used to carry out illegal or harmful activities such as sending spam, stealing sensitive data, initiating ransomware attacks, committing ad fraud through fraudulent clicks, or launching distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks.
- [AWS WAF – Application layer DDoS protection](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/aws-waf-antiddos-amr.md): You can use the AWS WAF AntiDDoS managed rule group (Anti-DDoS AMR) to protect your web applications against DDoS events in one place when using AWS WAF.
- [Third-party firewalls on Amazon EC2](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/third-party-firewalls.md): While AWS WAF is the recommended solution, many customers have third-party firewalls that they want to keep using when they move to the cloud.
- [AWS Shield Advanced Features](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/aws-shield-advanced-features.md): Managed DDoS event response with Shield Response Team support


## [Attack surface reduction](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/attack-surface-reduction.md)

### [Obfuscating AWS resources (BP1, BP4, BP5)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/obfuscating-aws-resources-bp1-bp4-bp5.md)

You can limit direct exposure of your backend resources by placing edge services—such as CloudFront, API Gateway, or load balancers—between your users and your origin infrastructure.

- [Security groups and network ACLs (BP5)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/security-groups-and-network-acls-bp5.md): Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC) allows you to provision a logically isolated section of the AWS Cloud where you can launch AWS resources in a virtual network that you define.
- [Protecting your origin (BP1, BP5)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/protecting-your-origin-bp1-bp5.md): When using Amazon CloudFront with an origin inside your VPC, it's crucial to ensure that only your CloudFront distribution can forward requests to your origin.
- [Protecting API endpoints (BP4)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/protecting-api-endpoints-bp4.md): When you must expose an API to the public, there's a risk that the API frontend could be targeted by a DDoS attack.


## [Operational techniques](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/operational-techniques.md)

- [Load testing](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/load-testing.md): Regularly load test your application using the guidelines in our Load Testing Applications whitepaper with both expected and above expected traffic levels so you can see how effective your architecture is, how your Auto Scaling policies function and how your error handling functions.

### [Metrics and alarms](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/metrics-and-alarms.md)

As a best practice you should be using infrastructure and application monitoring tools to check the availability of your application to help detect whether your application is being impacted by a DDoS event.

- [AWS WAF traffic overview dashboard](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/waf-traffic-overview-dashboard.md): The traffic overview dashboard in AWS WAF displays an overview of security-focused metrics so that you can identify and act on security risks in a few clicks, such as adding rate-based rules during DDoS events.

### [Logging](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/logging.md)

Enable useful logging on all services according to our logging and monitoring guide for application owners to maximize visibility and assist with troubleshooting.

- [Log correlation for DDoS resilient architectures](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/log-correlation-for-ddos-resilient-architectures.md): For DDoS resilient architectures, it's crucial to be able to correlate the logs between the services in the scope.
- [Visibility and protection management across multiple accounts](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/visibility-and-protection-management-across-multiple-accounts.md): In scenarios when you operate across multiple AWS accounts and have multiple components to protect, use techniques that enable you to operate at scale and reduce operational overhead to increase your mitigation capabilities.
- [Incident response strategy and runbooks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/incident-response-strategyrunbooks.md): Developing a DDoS attack incident response strategy and building a security incident response process around it is crucial for all organizations.
- [Support](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/support.md): If you experience an attack, you can also benefit from support from AWS in assessing the threat and reviewing the architecture of your application, or you might want to request other assistance.


